{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris","id":1450257,"title":"Children of Osiris","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-29T19:38:01-07:00","id":1450257,"title":"Children of Osiris","content":"A very long argument.<br /> <br /> Do the Children of Osiris add anything to V:TM or are they redundant with the Salubri?<br /> <br /> What would make them useful to a game of personal horror?<br /> <br /> How have you ever used them, if you have?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T20:13:12-07:00","id":1450259,"title":"","content":"I've never heard of anyone using the Children of Osiris. <br /> <br /> My best guess is that whichever writer created the Setites loved them so much that they put a heroic monster who fights the Setites in every game line that they could until an editor physically restrained them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T20:17:29-07:00","id":1450261,"title":"","content":"Are you familiar to what happened to them in Mummy: the Resurrection? They got resurrected. As in, they're human now; a cult of former vampires who serve Osiris in aiding the Reborn. <br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Salubri were wiped out a long time ago. Unless something has changed recently (which is possible; I don't follow Vampire all that closely).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T20:21:17-07:00","id":1450262,"title":"","content":"I've used them as a Golconda cult mostly seeming to show up in the late Enlightenment/early Victorian era, usually with the suspicion that their origins and the story of Osiris actually being a vampire are slightly suspect. However, their collection of unique blood magic rituals (as opposed to a full discipline) known as Duat (as opposed to the Tibetan Bardo), based on the idea that their undead existence is akin to traveling through the underworld and facing trials before a potential rebirth, is somewhat effective at temporarily reigning in the Beast and shoring up Humanity.<br /> <br /> But I also tend to use the Salubri a bit differently, having them be about as numerous as most Bloodlines, and scattered about in various drips and drabs that include Golconda seekers, Infernalists, those lurking hidden within the Camarilla, self serving cult leaders, soul-eaters, witch-hunters, would be good Samaritans, Noddist scholars, Sufi mystics, and so forth, so there's not much overlap between the two.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T21:17:15-07:00","id":1450271,"title":"","content":"I was inspired by the fact Keminitiri has levels of Bardo and is passionately devoted to the destruction of Set. It occurred to me the Children of Osiris become much more interesting if we assume that they were created by said 4th generation vampire as a weapon against the Followers of Set. Rather than a sincerely &quot;good&quot; group of vampires, they're really a very highly focused death cult that is being manipulated by an ancient Elder as a tool.<br /> <br /> They have no answers but DO have powerful tools (created by their founder) that make the Beast easier to cope with while the cult brainwashes them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T22:01:25-07:00","id":1450282,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I just automatically take it as a given that every vampire group is a bit shady on some level.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T22:05:46-07:00","id":1450283,"title":"","content":"I'm actually of a similar mind to CTPhipps, albeit I never noticed that intriguing facet of Kemintiri. But similarly I envision the Children less as bringers of salvation for the Kindred than as remorseless crusaders quite ready to strike down, embalm and seal away any kindred or ghouled mortal they deem beyond the pale of their strict ethics. I see them as less &quot;here are Good Guys(tm) with the Guaranteed Good Guy(tm) Power,&quot; and more, &quot;here's an order of Kindred which looks attractive but is as relentless in its way as the Setites they oppose.&quot; The dramatic potential increases if you consider the end-state of Bardo a kind of false Golconda, as I tend to---one which might look at first blush like everything a Kindred could hope for but is ultimately more brittle and limited than the hard road to enlightenment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26710-ventrue-busboy","name":"Ventrue Busboy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26710_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T23:07:07-07:00","id":1450284,"title":"","content":"My problem was always with the Children of Osiris as a sort of alternative to the Inconnu and Bardo as a sort of alternative to Golconda. But the Inconnu and Golconda themselves were always a hot mess and never really got suitably explored in the first place. So having these alternatives just felt really unnecessary. I think I'd like the Children of Osiris a lot more if Golconda had been better explored to begin with, so that an alternative felt refreshing and viable. Instead it just ends up feeling like a waltz where the two ideas just end up awkwardly stepping all over one another's toes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-29T23:25:36-07:00","id":1450285,"title":"","content":"I think having the Children be a cult manipulated by an evil methuselah is somewhat overdone. If you want to play up the &quot;they're not actually good guys&quot; angle, I'd look towards Cairo by Night. Osiris killed most of the Children for being moral hypocrites who used Bardo to indulge their Beasts while insisting they were good guys. <br /> <br /> I feel like this character type gets overlooked in Masquerade a lot. There can be vampires who think of themselves as good people when they're actually terrible people. They don't just have to be jaded assholes who accept they're bad actors in a bad world or ravening Sabbat monsters.<br /> <br /> Khalid from Chicago 1e was a good example of this. He genuinely did value morality and spent centuries seeking Golconda. He just... wasn't very good at it. He never mastered his Beast, for all that he might have wanted to, and he still Embraced incestuous brother-sister preteen couples and believed he was doing a good thing. <br /> <br /> Obviously, Osiris killing off the Children doesn't give GMs very much ability to use them in games. So they could still be around, a cult of moral hypocrites seeking Golconda, purporting to serve Osiris, or whatever other holy cause have you, and unable to grasp they'll never get there. In fact, perhaps their Bardo Discipline actually <i>is</i> very effective at staving off the Beat... just too effective. The Children might kill a few mortals, rationalize the deaths as necessary, make a show of repentance, and then use Bardo to restore their Humanity, rinse and repeat. They've let the Discipline become a crutch to substitute for the actual hard spiritual work Golconda demands. Vampires who come across the Children think they're saints when they're actually just very good at lying to themselves.<br /> <br /> High Humanity can be its own Masquerade. <br /> <br /> <br /> Alternatively, for a more good guy-centric approach, I also like No One of Consequence's idea of them as a Golconda cult with an Egyptian focus and unique Blood Sorcery rituals tied to the Underworld. There's no need to make them a bloodline. Their inability to create childer could just be a mystic rite that inductees subject themselves to as a sign of their devotion to Golconda, since they believe the Embrace to be utterly immoral. <br /> <br /> <br /> I think the setting has enough bad guys and evil cults, honestly, that I'd probably make them a mix of the above two ideas if I ever used them in a game. They are a Golconda cult with some sincere seekers, but they have some morally hypocritical bad apples too, and Bardo is retarding their spiritual progress as much as helping it. They're flawed but well-intentioned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T01:00:12-07:00","id":1450289,"title":"","content":"I've never actually used the Children of Osiris in my chronicles, but they are a background element out there that might be encountered if I ever had PCs interested in doing something that I feel fits well with them.<br /> <br /> I use them as their own small sect of vampires, and not actually a bloodline. Membership in the sect grants you access to the Bardo Discipline.<br /> <br /> Their main reason for existence in my version of the setting is to provide an alternative to Golconda. The disadvantage is that it's just an artificial means of preserving Humanity. The advantage is that it works for practical purposes while Golconda is almost never achieved by its seekers. Their secondary reason is to act as allies, mentors, or support for PCs who would fight the Setites or other vampires who actively serve the powers of Darkness - followers of the Path of Evil Revelation, Baali, or other servants of the Wyrm in its many manifestations. They are somewhat high level players of the Jyhad, but only in a limited capacity. The mummy Horus doesn't really trust them, but uses them as unreliable allies/pawns when he communicates with the Grand Undying King. They're basically &quot;ancient vampire mystics&quot;.<br /> <br /> I also have some other aspects to the Children. First, they are a repository of a lot of occult knowledge besides Bardo, so they have access to many of the ancient pre-Tremere thaumaturgical paths. Second, Osiris himself was a Cappadocian, either a childe of Cappadocious himself or of one of his childer. So the Children have a number of Cappadocians that survived the purge (it's kind of an open secret in my setting that there was no way the Giovanni could have destroyed all the Cappadocians, but it is true they are very rare). So PCs who are interested in those aspects might somehow run into them, but the Children would be much warier of such vampires as such interests have nothing to do with the purposes of the sect.<br /> <br /> None of my players have ever been interested in anything that might result in them crossing paths or swords with the Children of Osiris so they've never come into play. But if I had a PC truly interested in restoring their humanity, becoming a major enemy of the Setites, researching lost lore that the Children preserve, or trying to learn about or become involved in the really high aspects of the Jyhad, I would use the Children. But the players of my games usually have much more mundane interests. In a sense, they exist so they can be a reward to those players who want to do something different that grow temporal power or become sucked into sect wars and internal sect politics.<br /> <br /> I am much less interested in the Salubri whom I consider to really be part of the Tremere metaplot, and the mystery behind them (I like things like the Tremere flaw that provides them with a Third Eye and other aspects that heighten the paranoia within the Tremere). I would never use Salubri anti-tribu in the Sabbat, and the Salubri bloodline doesn't interest me much. Unlike the Children, I would probably never use one in a chronicle unless a PC was really, really interested in them and put in the effort to justify me putting work into them. <br /> <br /> Salubri are one of many first edition concepts which I was less than impressed with when they were expanded upon later. While the Dark Ages Salubri clanbook has some interesting things in it that expanded the clan, I also think it diverged from certain key aspects of the original bloodline description. In so far there was a split within the clan, it's less Warrior vs Healer than those embraced prior to Saulot achieving Golconda (pretty much every Salubri destroyed by the Tremere or as a result of their lives) and those embraced afterwards (who were the basis of the modern bloodline as we know them). Saulot pretty much abandoned his original childer when he created the new ones post-Golconda. For many practical purposes, they were two different clans despite having the same founder.<br /> <br /> While Golconda is an important aspect of the Salubri, for metagame purposes in my chronicle, they're only purpose is if someone is really, really interested in the mystery of Saulot himself, his intentions, and his legacy. So they do not occupy the same space as the Children of Osiris in my chronicles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T01:42:23-07:00","id":1450291,"title":"","content":"Osiris judging the Children for their crimes is about as moral sounding as Cappadocias doing it. He's a 4th or 3rd generation vampire himself so he has no room to judge.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T03:04:04-07:00","id":1450295,"title":"","content":"The Children of Osiris have never made an appearance in my games, but if they did, I would simply use them as written in <i>Hunters Hunted.</i> They were originally presented as a tiny sect (not a bloodline) of vampire hunters. That answers the question &quot;What would make them useful to a game of personal horror?&quot; to my satisfaction. They fit in a game of personal horror like any other Hunter group -- either as antagonists to the PCs, or as uneasy allies with the PCs against some Big Bad.<br /> <br /> The Children are not really redundant with the Salubri, because the latter are the <i>hunted,</i> not the hunters. If anything, the Children might be redundant with <i>Mummies</i> -- they fill the same narrative role as foils for the Followers of Set. However, I think that a group of vampiric Buddhist contemplatives is different enough from a group of immortal Egyptian sorcerers for the World of Darkness to have room for both, so the overlap is not a problem for me.<br /> <br /> I've never been keen on the fandom's apparent hostility toward genuine good-guy groups like the Children of Osiris and the Salubri. I see no reason to turn either into secret assholes, because they function perfectly alright as written -- as tiny rays of light in the World of Darkness that the player characters might stumble across on a rare occasion. A setting where <i>absolutely everyone</i> is some shade of hypocritical dickhead is boring and samey to me. You need rays of light to make the darkness deeper, too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm","name":"Elphilm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"763"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T03:22:01-07:00","id":1450296,"title":"","content":"Never used them. But I appreciate them from a lore point of view.<br /> <br /> I always thought kindred history from antiquity and backwards was a bit skimpy.I like th notion that in the dim past there were also fallen clans and bloodlines going extinct, Antediluvians that didn't make it, etc. If I were to use the Children, they would be some fragments of ancient history. An (supposedly) extinct bloodline with unusual lore. Unlike other chtonic bits of forgotten knowledge, this would have been explorations of humanity, ways of chaining the Beast, and blunting the Curse. If I had involved that in a chronicle, the Children would have turned out not to be extinct but not what the legens said either. Probably tied to the area arond their pillars to start with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1450271#post1450271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was inspired by the fact Keminitiri has levels of Bardo and is passionately devoted to the destruction of Set. It occurred to me the Children of Osiris become much more interesting if we assume that they were created by said 4th generation vampire as a weapon against the Followers of Set. Rather than a sincerely &quot;good&quot; group of vampires, they're really a very highly focused death cult that is being manipulated by an ancient Elder as a tool.<br /> <br /> They have no answers but DO have powerful tools (created by their founder) that make the Beast easier to cope with while the cult brainwashes them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thats canon.<br /> <br /> Osiris was a horrible creature, probably would have been a wight within 50 or even 25 years as a vampire if he had been allowed to continue his existence. Caitiff of the Trujah line. Khetamon masqueraded as him after Osiris met final death and did all the stuff Osiris is supposed to have done. Kheminitri was a member of the Cult of Isis in her breathing days. After she was embraced the Children of Osiris sought her out and pulled her out of wassail (only time that has ever happened as far as I know). She joined the Children for a while, and then had a falling out with them. The Children of Osiris took the safe passage to Rome offered by Mithras in the first century B.C.<br /> <br /> They fell some unspecified amount of time later, after Mithras left for Britannia.<br /> <br /> Kheminitri reformed the Children of Osiris as tool centered on her in the 1300s.<br /> <br /> Interestingly, when Osiris/Khetamon wiped of many of the Children and returned the rest in the modern day, he exempted Kheminitri, Horus old girlfriend.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2471-trollroot","name":"Trollroot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"311"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T06:39:28-07:00","id":1450309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1450291#post1450291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Osiris judging the Children for their crimes is about as moral sounding as Cappadocias doing it. He's a 4th or 3rd generation vampire himself so he has no room to judge.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was under the impression that Osiris was killed and turned into a wraith, and he also punishes Mummies when they step out of line and engage in bad behavior. But it's been awhile since I read through the Mummy book. Maybe he was just a vampire who hung out in the Underworld and did that?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 05-30-2021, 09:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T10:44:02-07:00","id":1450329,"title":"","content":"I like the Ancient Egyptian theme of them. I find Bardo to be a problem as I often do many rando unique Disciplines. It has balance issues for sure. One good way to use them different than cannon is to make them a full Clan and have the two openly oppose each other, rather than the Followers of Set simply winning out. Mummy is one of the few supernaturals that mesh rules wise easy with VTM being a simple supplement of it. You could set the game in Egypt or anywhere really. I enjoy all the backstory even if I have not used them outside of 1 short run back in the 90s when they first came out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T12:38:31-07:00","id":1450343,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elphilm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1450295#post1450295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Children are not really redundant with the Salubri, because the latter are the <i>hunted,</i> not the hunters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Excellent point. If you retain that, the differences in approach to the two groups by the ST should become very clear. Disappointed I didn't articulate that myself.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T11:14:30-07:00","id":1450471,"title":"","content":"As far as I remember, he was killed and resurrected by Isis. The process made him &quot;other&quot;. He's no truly vampiric nor wraith and has godlike power at least in his realm","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T14:26:58-07:00","id":1450505,"title":"","content":"I don't know about personal horror but they are very useful in more general Urban Fantasy games and are on of the few elements of the overall WoD setting that don't get bent out of shape in crossovers so they are also useful in that context. They do work better a npcs then pcs in my opinion. The kind of mysterious hidden cult of &quot;good guy&quot; vamps who can provide the pcs with hidden kknowledge but not directly help them due to passivism/isolationaism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15685-up-to-eleven","name":"Up-to-Eleven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T17:56:24-07:00","id":1450522,"title":"","content":"One of the reasons I enjoy using the Children in the Victorian period is that they get to ride that real world wave of Egyptomania that started in the Napoleonic period and continued up until the 1930s, including its various influences on European mystical circles. By the time of the original game (the 1990s), I imagine the majority of Ancilla members look very much like Victorian throwbacks in their rites and rituals.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T12:04:14-07:00","id":1450621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1450522#post1450522\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the reasons I enjoy using the Children in the Victorian period is that they get to ride that real world wave of Egyptomania that started in the Napoleonic period and continued up until the 1930s, including its various influences on European mystical circles. By the time of the original game (the 1990s), I imagine the majority of Ancilla members look very much like Victorian throwbacks in their rites and rituals.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it would definitely provide them an opportunity to expand their network of temples through a front organization(s). <br /> <br /> Then again, their original grand temple is in Nepal and they named their Discipline after a tenet of Buddhism which post dates them by a long, long time (actually I hold most of the terms used in the game are there simply for the ease of playing an RPG and may not accurately reflect the true IC terms used for these concepts, so I don't worry too much about it and tell my players to do the same). So we shouldn't hold the Children too hard as keeping the trappings of Egypt. Just like any other vampires, they hide their true nature and adapt to hide within whatever human culture surrounds them. Important to remember this applies to the Setites too.<br /> <br /> But sometimes you just want to take advantage of something because it's cool to do so. And it is probably easier to recruit mortal hunters of Setite vampires among people already interested in Egyptology and &quot;know&quot; Set is a bad guy they should oppose than some random goober with no pre-built interest in them. I don't think the Children would actually seek to embrace anyone, just recruit existing vampires, but they should have allies (let's not call them pawns for politeness sake) among mortals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T12:10:52-07:00","id":1450622,"title":"","content":"Oh yeah. Its a pretty important that the Children of Osiris don't embrace. They are vampires that become good by rejecting the vampire state of being. Not like the traditional idea of Golconda in-universe of growing beyond vampirism through enlightenment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15685-up-to-eleven","name":"Up-to-Eleven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-17T18:36:14-07:00","id":1455089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Up-to-Eleven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1450622#post1450622\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh yeah. Its a pretty important that the Children of Osiris don't embrace. They are vampires that become good by rejecting the vampire state of being. Not like the traditional idea of Golconda in-universe of growing beyond vampirism through enlightenment.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, the Children of Osiris' path is one of eternal struggle through self-denial and asceticism in contrast with Golconda's mytheme of transcending the conflict between Man and Beast that is part of all kindred.<br /> <br /> <br /> Curiously, the one time i have used the Children of Osiris was with a member just out of torpor becoming something of father figure to a domain's Caitiffs and thin-bloods, giving them a sort of support structure, guidance and sense of identity through the cult's history and teaching of Bardo to help in bulwarking their Humanity. What ironically also gave him a far from insignificant amount of pull among the city's kindred, specially the anarchs and some disgruntled young licks in the lower rungs of the status quo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 07-17-2021, 09:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-17T23:19:26-07:00","id":1455094,"title":"","content":"Never got round to using them, but I imagine it'd be a blast to slaughter such self-righteous pricks. Glory to Set! <br /> <br /> For reals though, consider the impact of using a ritual to restore humanity. Every time you do a bad thing, you just need to prostrate yourself before a cow and devour it's awful blood. Then you're fine. Theft? Cow of forgiveness. Murder? Cow of forgiveness. Rape? Cow of Forgiveness. Genocide? Cow of forgiveness. What kind of vampire results from such a masochistic pardon for all sins? How liberating is that kind of assurance? It doesn't bode well. For this reason, the Camarilla are going to be harder on CoO than the Followers of Set. Of course some CoO will be genuinely &quot;nice&quot; vampires, but the ritual is designed to be abused, and it'd have horrific consequences if it ever spread. <br /> <br /> <br /> In terms of mechanics. I treat Bardo as a collection of rituals for Akhu (settite sorcery). I don't like disciplines like Bardo or Ogham which are really just collections of rituals (Ogham gets folded into koldunic sorcery). The &quot;you must be this humane to use X&quot; system makes more sense as a component of thaumaturgy than it does as a Discipline in any case. There is no CoO bloodline, it's just a cult.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T00:34:48-07:00","id":1455095,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455094#post1455094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For reals though, consider the impact of using a ritual to restore humanity. Every time you do a bad thing, you just need to prostrate yourself before a cow and devour it's awful blood.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with your sentiment that the use of a ritual to restore humanity is problematic, but I think it's important to keep a few things in mind. It sounds like you actually know the rules for Bardo so you are using exaggeration for effect, but people who don't know the rules may think you are being serious, so I'll make some comments for them.<br /> <br /> First, the power that allows you to restore Humanity requires a Conscience roll (with the difficulty being the Humanity level trying to be regained), so in effect it is a &quot;do over&quot; roll for a character's failure. It's limited in how frequently you can do it (within one week of losing that level). So it's not like you are able to shotgun regaining humanity. It's a one time &quot;do over&quot; to make you feel guilty like you should have felt that first time but you rolled bad. At the cost of burning all your blood in V20 (but not the original 1e Hunters Hunted).<br /> <br /> Second, by &quot;cow of forgiveness&quot; I assume you mean the third level of Bardo, Gift of Apis. That just allows you to feed on animal blood as if it were as nutritious as human. So there's not actually any cow of forgiveness. But I find your terminology funny.<br /> <br /> Having said that, yes, there's a problematic aspect to the idea of &quot;I did something bad, but I have an easy way out&quot; that you are depicting. I don't think Bardo is actually like that. But there is a potential for abuse. But given the limits of Bardo one, I think the potential is minor. And within the context of the Children of Osiris philosophy, it's likely not abused much. But if that ever got out into the general population, your concerns would be justified. I could see any number of vampire jerks using it in a cheap way to ward off abuse, as opposed to part of a spiritual discipline for a true penitent. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In terms of mechanics. I treat Bardo as a collection of rituals for Akhu (settite sorcery). I don't like disciplines like Bardo or Ogham which are really just collections of rituals (Ogham gets folded into koldunic sorcery). </div> </div> </div>I do agree with this. There are a lot of Disciplines out there for various bloodlines that are essentially a hodgepodge of various powers combined to create a type of flavor for that bloodline. Doesn't make much sense from an IC perspective. Sometimes it seems they could be made a kind of Blood Sorcery Path, but other times it is really just rituals as you said. If you have the time, probably the best thing you can do is to recreate the entire thing as a system of rituals or perhaps a path of Blood Sorcery (or both). For Bardo, you are probably right - this is best seen as a series of Rituals. Which means the Children of Osiris should have at least one Path of blood magic that allows them to perform rituals. As such, I agree that looking at Akhu would be best, and the least morally problematic Path would be Ushabti. Which is a power also shared with Mummy's Hekau. So it makes sense thematically for the Children of Osiris.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T01:57:26-07:00","id":1455098,"title":"","content":"&quot;cow of forgiveness&quot; is really a side-effect of the ritual. If I recall correctly Bardo 1 requires a sacrifice of a lot of blood, enough to put most vampires into starvation-frenzy mode, so you're going to want to eat straight after you go through the ritual. Thus having a cow or something on standby is rather essential for most high-gen characters.<br /> Naturally, throwing a bunch of humans in front of you would be dangerous and very counter to what you're doing the ritual for, so shitty animal blood is the way. <br /> <br /> Does the &quot;animal blood is useful&quot; three-dot power that i forgot about make the blood more palletable? <br /> <br /> <br /> I think even if you're a &quot;good&quot; vampire and start using Bardo for the right reasons, eventually you'll realise you can do it for the wrong reasons. You're not going to be butchering people for fun on the regular, but &quot;it's just one murder, I've got a ritual for it&quot; is a very slippery slope. Once your faith affirms your evil indulgences, it really will drag you towards depravity. Bardo reinforces the idea that you're a good person even as you do bad things. You'll sin, you'll do some self suffering and that'll wipe away your sin. Congratulations; you've just abused a child for fun and then earned for yourself some forgiveness; That's a possitive feedback loop. That's something that puts the moral locus of control on you: You can sin, but you can also forgive yourself. For a lot of people, that's a fast route to crazy town. <br /> <br /> Bardo really is a bunch of rituals (banishing sign is more path like, but there are precedents for instant-rituals, like the vampire equivalent of jam technology.) and there are 6+ powers for it. But as to what primary path CoO will pick... I'm unsure. Part of me thinks they'll still go for the Path of Blood. Or, in true anti-settite fashion, they'll go for Lure of Flames (which Settites presumably avoid like the plague; the gods associated with fire are Set's enemies, and the brightness of the fire is going to upset their bloodline weakness) There are many good options. I don't think the CoO necessarily need to be paired with a &quot;good&quot; path, but they are unlikely to pick corruption.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T04:36:07-07:00","id":1455102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455095#post1455095\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I agree with your sentiment that the use of a ritual to restore humanity is problematic, but I think it's important to keep a few things in mind. It sounds like you actually know the rules for Bardo so you are using exaggeration for effect, but people who don't know the rules may think you are being serious, so I'll make some comments for them.<br /> <br /> First, the power that allows you to restore Humanity requires a Conscience roll (with the difficulty being the Humanity level trying to be regained), so in effect it is a &quot;do over&quot; roll for a character's failure. It's limited in how frequently you can do it (within one week of losing that level). So it's not like you are able to shotgun regaining humanity. It's a one time &quot;do over&quot; to make you feel guilty like you should have felt that first time but you rolled bad. At the cost of burning all your blood in V20 (but not the original 1e Hunters Hunted).<br /> <br /> Second, by &quot;cow of forgiveness&quot; I assume you mean the third level of Bardo, Gift of Apis. That just allows you to feed on animal blood as if it were as nutritious as human. So there's not actually any cow of forgiveness. But I find your terminology funny.<br /> <br /> Having said that, yes, there's a problematic aspect to the idea of &quot;I did something bad, but I have an easy way out&quot; that you are depicting. I don't think Bardo is actually like that. But there is a potential for abuse. But given the limits of Bardo one, I think the potential is minor. And within the context of the Children of Osiris philosophy, it's likely not abused much. But if that ever got out into the general population, your concerns would be justified. I could see any number of vampire jerks using it in a cheap way to ward off abuse, as opposed to part of a spiritual discipline for a true penitent.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would further argue that, by nature of definition, the kind of headspace and conscious self-reflection necessary to even use Bardo 1 would, more often than not, preclude someone from knowingly using it as &quot;an easy way out&quot;. The character is deliberately making themselves feel bad about their actions. It requires a cultivation of Conscience that is directly at odds with a blase attitudes towards morality. Quite the opposite, in fact.<br /> <br /> If you feel bad enough about what you've done to claw back the Humanity you lost, you'd also feel bad about abusing that power to indulge your every desire. The Humanity stat is not just a meter dictating how much control you have over the Beast. It's a measure of your capacity to think and feel about something other than yourself.<br /> <br /> This doesn't mean a Child of Osiris wouldn't get into a situation where they were regularly vacillating between committing Sins and using Restore Humanitas. That can absolutely happen. It's just they'd be less an uncaring person using it as a crutch, and more just bad at sticking to their principles.<br /> <br /> In short, they're a person, subject to normal person moral failings. Always trying to be better, even if they don't always succeed. Just like all of us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T05:30:19-07:00","id":1455103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elphilm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1450295#post1450295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Children of Osiris have never made an appearance in my games, but if they did, I would simply use them as written in <i>Hunters Hunted.</i> They were originally presented as a tiny sect (not a bloodline) of vampire hunters. That answers the question &quot;What would make them useful to a game of personal horror?&quot; to my satisfaction. They fit in a game of personal horror like any other Hunter group -- either as antagonists to the PCs, or as uneasy allies with the PCs against some Big Bad.<br /> <br /> The Children are not really redundant with the Salubri, because the latter are the <i>hunted,</i> not the hunters. If anything, the Children might be redundant with <i>Mummies</i> -- they fill the same narrative role as foils for the Followers of Set. However, I think that a group of vampiric Buddhist contemplatives is different enough from a group of immortal Egyptian sorcerers for the World of Darkness to have room for both, so the overlap is not a problem for me.<br /> <br /> I've never been keen on the fandom's apparent hostility toward genuine good-guy groups like the Children of Osiris and the Salubri. I see no reason to turn either into secret assholes, because they function perfectly alright as written -- as tiny rays of light in the World of Darkness that the player characters might stumble across on a rare occasion. A setting where <i>absolutely everyone</i> is some shade of hypocritical dickhead is boring and samey to me. You need rays of light to make the darkness deeper, too.</div> </div> </div> </div>^This. Nothing is more boring than every faction in the setting being slight variations of the same asshole with different coats of paint.<br /> <br /> I LIKE the Children of Osiris and the Salubri being bright spots in an otherwise grimdark game. It keeps things interesting. If every faction is full of dishonest, craven bastards, the players will learn to just expect to be betrayed by everyone they deal with. And thus will stop dealing with them honestly, and instead always work to kill or betray or manipulate everyone first. Like a D&amp;D party that learns to crawl over a dungeon with a ten foot pole, checking every inch for deathtraps, it gets tedious fast when they expect every social encounter to be a trap.<br /> <br /> As for how to use the Children of Osiris, I think the tendency to not know what to do with them is a result of being in an intellectual rut. Storytellers are set firmly in a certain VtM comfort zone. Where there are always wheels within wheels, everyone is looking for an advantage, and will stoop to whatever low to get what they want for petty reasons. That mortals and mortal institutions must be lowercase-d dominated, in order to get a leg up over the vampire's rivals. With Vampire: The Masquerade, every NPC or faction is expected to do these things, to some degree, because that's the mode we've come to expect from vampires.<br /> <br /> Consider, then, what kind of stories could be told when you factor in a faction that does not do these things. Not because they're naive or pure, but because they actively choose a different path. If a Child of Osiris gets involved in the Jyhad, it's because of motivations completely different from other vampires. Nor do they use many of the most common tools and tactics, because they are evil. They instead employ tactics a normal vampire never would, for reasons a normal vampire would never consider. Partly this is out of necessity, because many avenues to get what they want are closed off to them by choice. But it also means the Children of Osiris have reason to become very good at using alternative means, because they're the only ones left. Even if they are less convenient or close off opportunities other vampires would relish.<br /> <br /> Osirans do not accumulate human herds, and thus have no human herds to attack. (They find it distasteful, and besides seek to overcome their reliance on human blood through Bardo 3). They don't seek to acquire normal Influence, because temporal power means little to them, and they have no interest in gaining power over mortals or other vampires. Jockeying for social position within the local Kindred hierarchy is meaningless to a sect that stands firmly outside such social structures and avoids even being known of whenever possible. They willing to make unlife harder for every vampire in the area (ex: attracting vampire hunters, improving conditions in the Rack, bringing attention to institutional corruption, etc), because they're used to deprivation and going it the hard way. If presented with the opportunity to claim an artifact or knowledge of great power for themselves, they're more likely to destroy it or spread it around, depending on the circumstances, if it means no one (or everyone) can benefit from it.<br /> <br /> All of this makes the Children of Osiris unpredictable to other vampires. In many ways a high Humanity vampire is constrained by their own ethics, in a way other vampires are not. In other ways, though, they are more free. They can affect the world around them by &quot;attacking&quot; from angles no &quot;sane&quot; vampire would consider an option. Either because it seems counterproductive, or because more expedient methods are available, or because they don't see the benefits of doing it. Some vampires would tie themselves up in mental knots, trying to figure out what the &quot;game&quot; is in a situation, because they cannot conceive of a vampire not acting the way every vampire should.<br /> <br /> The Children of Osiris have the advantage of not &quot;making sense&quot;, because despite them being on Humanity, same as most vampires by default, they have a radically different way of thinking. They go against everything other Kindred value in the Jyhad.<br /> <br /> So when examining the Children of Osiris and their potential role in the game, we shouldn't ask how we can change them to fit into pre-existing molds. Instead, we should ask ourselves &quot;What can the Children of Osiris do that other groups cannot or would not? And how do we leverage that into unique plot hooks?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T06:08:35-07:00","id":1455104,"title":"","content":"You are artificially making yourself feel bad. With Magic. I don't see why you would need &quot;organic&quot; feelings to pull this spell off. It's like saying you need to actually love the vampire in a mundane fashion before you can be bloodbound. <br /> <br /> &quot;conscience&quot; can be a weird thing. You can meet people who wouldn't bat an eye at -reallyBadThing- and yet would feel severe guilt or outrage from -NotReallyThatBad-. Without going to much into a discussion on the nature of the &quot;Humanity&quot; meter, If the children of Osiris were genuinely, purely good folks, they wouldn't need the ritual in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T06:43:53-07:00","id":1455106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455104#post1455104\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You are artificially making yourself feel bad. With Magic. I don't see why you would need &quot;organic&quot; feelings to pull this spell off. It's like saying you need to actually love the vampire in a mundane fashion before you can be bloodbound.</div> </div> </div> </div>A distinction without a difference. Whether the feelings are &quot;organic&quot; or &quot;artificial&quot;, they're still &quot;real&quot; in the sense that you feel them.<br /> <br /> This is even assuming Bardo works as you claim. Rather than a blood-empowered form of meditation that forces oneself to confront the callousness of one's actions, and thereby undo the Beast's tightening grip on your soul. For all we know, the emotions it inspires could be extremely &quot;organic&quot;. Albeit &quot;tool assisted&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;conscience&quot; can be a weird thing. You can meet people who wouldn't bat an eye at -reallyBadThing- and yet would feel severe guilt or outrage from -NotReallyThatBad-. </div> </div> </div>On this, we are in agreement.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Without going to much into a discussion on the nature of the &quot;Humanity&quot; meter, If the children of Osiris were genuinely, purely good folks, they wouldn't need the ritual in the first place. </div> </div> </div>The fact that they go to great lengths to join the Children of Osiris, learn a whole Discipline, and put that Discipline into practice says a great deal about their intentions.<br /> <br /> Moreover, it's misleading to think in terms of them &quot;being good&quot;. Goodness is not a state of being - save possibly for the divine, if any - but rather action carried out. The nobility of the Children of Osiris comes from the constant, active refusal of the Beast's urges, and the exercise of control. It is in doing good, and not doing evil. The latter being contrary to the vampire's nature. There is no &quot;being good&quot;. It's a lifestyle.<br /> <br /> True, any vampire could maintain a high Humanity without Bardo. But that is, as I've described it before, Vampire Hard Mode. Maintaining Humanity is very difficult, and losing it very easy. It's not fair to judge a character's goodness by them never erring. Circumstances sometimes make it impossible to avoid breaking some tenet of Humanity, or else making the price of not doing so unreasonably high (up to and including both Final Death and A Fate Worse Than Death).<br /> <br /> I do not, therefore, consider it a mark against the Children of Osiris that they practice an ascetic discipline (or Discipline) to help them not spiral into moral decay, Wassail, and Wight-dom. If anything, I would say the willingness to become a Child of Osiris - with all that this entails - is proof of their sincerity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T09:44:29-07:00","id":1455113,"title":"","content":"Imagine you give someone, and to make up for it you cut yourself. Or perhaps you steal a million dollars from a place that needs it, and later choose to give some of that money to a charity.You didn't feel bad enough at the time, but you can sit down and have a thing about it and recover from the loss of not feeling bad. Cheating &quot;humanity&quot; is not humane.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T11:52:02-07:00","id":1455121,"title":"","content":"I'm going to hop in on the side of &quot;Bardo isn't just a cheat-code for Humanity that invites abuse.&quot; I think partly it has to do with the fact that we are reflecting on a game mechanic, and it is easy to look at it as abusable from that perspective, like many game mechanics. But as people have noted, getting a Conscience re-roll, In Character, ought to represent authentic moral reflection. If the game mechanic is being abused, the Storyteller ought to intervene. But I don't think that the Discipline, In Character, invites the kind of abuse you describe, or represents an especially pernicious way for people to do bad things and avoid the psychological/spiritual consequences. This is a game where Elders creating childer and grandchilder to do dirty work for them in order to avoid Humanity loss is considered a feature of the setting; Bardo is just a more personal and mystical way of achieving the same result. Except it's also an occult secret held by a group of religious ascetics who make it their business to destroy other vampires that fail to live up to the standards of their cult. I also don't think that it being &quot;artificial&quot; is especially problematic. The vampires in question might struggle with Humanity, but they clearly care enough to try and maintain it, and I don't think seeking out some external support invalidates the effort. I think an analogy might be made to mental illness; some people need a supportive community to make it work, some people might need medication to handle their worst symptoms. Bardo is a spiritual prescription against the Beast, from that perspective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T14:34:36-07:00","id":1455128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455113#post1455113\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Imagine you give someone, and to make up for it you cut yourself. Or perhaps you steal a million dollars from a place that needs it, and later choose to give some of that money to a charity.You didn't feel bad enough at the time, but you can sit down and have a thing about it and recover from the loss of not feeling bad. Cheating &quot;humanity&quot; is not humane.</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno, I think there's something to be said for the fact that the Children of Osiris are nothing but a bunch of self-brainwashing cultists who have thrown themselves off the Path of Golconda and magically bind themselves to Humanity rather than try to succeed on their own merits. Then again, I very much think the best use for them is to make them Kemintiri's puppets and people who are deeply deluded about their own morality.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T18:51:53-07:00","id":1455152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455103#post1455103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So when examining the Children of Osiris and their potential role in the game, we shouldn't ask how we can change them to fit into pre-existing molds. Instead, we should ask ourselves &quot;What can the Children of Osiris do that other groups cannot or would not? And how do we leverage that into unique plot hooks?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that is a really good angle to use as an ST. And you brought up a lot of good points (as did Elphilim) on the most salient aspects of the Children. In short, they are active participants in the Jyhad and taking out their defined enemies (the Setites mostly, but probably some of the most inhuman Sabbat on certain Paths of Enlightenment, and possibly some individuals of the Camarilla) but from the other side of the Humanity trait than what we're used to. I like the idea that their effectively a wild card in vampire society because so much of what they do ignores what are supposed to be the defining marks of vampires.<br /> <br /> An issue for many STs is that after some of the excesses of 1e and early 2e, they are keen not to have &quot;vampire superheroes&quot; who effectively ignore the damnation of being undead, or used to pull the PCs bacon out of the fire. So that has to be avoided. But an easy mistake to then make is to make them stereotypical tortured souls, which is another trope that is easily overused, or a mere plot device to rescue the PCs from their enemies. So getting the portrayal right is a minefield. <br /> <br /> A good example might be like monks in the Dark Ages desperately preserving the tattered remnants of lost knowledge in a more barbarous age. In this case though, the knowledge is the teachings of Osiris. And not just preserving them, but setting an example for others. They probably have some knowledge of Golconda and meet a lot of prospects in Golconda seekers, but would present it as a practical issue (&quot;We don't know if Golconda is true, but we do know that Bardo - and the teachings of Osiris - work&quot;.)<br /> <br /> I think one of the keys in their portrayal is that they are an extremely tiny sect with few members. Therefore while they are a danger to their enemies, they can be easily crushed and therefore need to minimize their exposure and maximize their secrecy. I would portray them as a hard target, but so few and so secret, their enemies are more likely to forget about them and move on. They are also helped in that they would be one of the few vampire sects with allies among other supernaturals.<br /> <br /> Also, they reason they &quot;hunt&quot; their enemies is not so much a proactive measure (i.e. we really don't like evil vampires and eliminate them because we're good), but a reactive one (i.e. there are a lot of other vampires that don't like what we represent and are trying to kill us, so we selectively kill them so we don't get eliminated ourselves.) They kill threats because they are in a war with dangerous enemies, not generic bad guys who don't know or care about them. That means if they ever show up in a chronicle, it should be for a sufficiently compelling reason for them. They shouldn't be there just to be there.<br /> <br /> So the question now becomes why are their Temples and Bardo a threat, and who seeks to kill them? Obviously the Setites are an enemy for historical reasons. This a blood feud. The Baali would be another likely enemy (the Baali are pretty much at war with everyone). <br /> <br /> Another enemy are those Paths of Enlightenment most opposed to what the Children of Osiris and their philosophy represent. Path of Evil Revelations is certainly among them. I would also consider adherents of the Path of Caine, Path of Blood, and Path of Night to see the Children of Osiris as infidels to be destroyed. Possibly the Path of Lilith, but I think that really depends on the kind of Lilith her followers envision. Some may not think of the Children as an enemy, merely deluded and ignore them so the Children would do the same. It really comes down to the intolerance level of the Path for vampires to choose to be something very different from them, and how dangerous they think the philosophy of Osiris represent to their teachings. <br /> <br /> The Giovanni or a faction within them might be another, if we consider Osiris as a wraith king in one of the Far Shores and/or Dark Kingdom. There is probably a Heretic group of Wraiths loyal to Osiris, and this group could run afoul of some plans of the Giovanni. Also the possibility that the Children might host some of the surviving Cappadocians who joined the cult earlier.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T22:22:26-07:00","id":1455170,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455152#post1455152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A good example might be like monks in the Dark Ages desperately preserving the tattered remnants of lost knowledge in a more barbarous age. In this case though, the knowledge is the teachings of Osiris. And not just preserving them, but setting an example for others. They probably have some knowledge of Golconda and meet a lot of prospects in Golconda seekers, but would present it as a practical issue (&quot;We don't know if Golconda is true, but we do know that Bardo - and the teachings of Osiris - work&quot;.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. Individual members of the Children of Osiris may have different views about Bardo. Not surprising, since they have a lot of time to meditate upon it, and contemplate its meaning.<br /> <br /> For some, Bardo is an end unto itself. This is why I disagree with CTPhipps about Osirans &quot;throwing themselves off the path of Golconda&quot;. The notion presupposes that Golconda is the only goal worth seeking. Rather, Bardo is a different path altogether, based on a different set of value judgements. If you merely desire to keep the Beast at bay without losing your Humanity, Bardo answers this need. Some vampires just want to continue being immortal, while doing as little harm as possible.<br /> <br /> For others, Bardo represents the ultimate stopgap measure. They may think that Golconda is possible, but that the possibility of achieving it does little good for them in the immediate sense. They're still vulnerable to degeneration, and they still must take blood from humans in order to survive. Unless or until Golconda is achieved - and possibly not even then - they must still contend with the Beast. Bardo insulates them from this threat, allowing them to maintain a minimal harm unlifestyle for the foreseeable future. Moreover, unlike Golconda - a state the books have always kept vague, mysterious, and mythical - we KNOW Bardo works. We have game mechanics for it. It's no big stretch to assume that the Children of Osiris are very aware of its efficacy.<br /> <br /> While the search for Golconda is ongoing, a Child of Osiris can use Bardo to buy time.<br /> <br /> There is, of course, the very real possibility that Bardo and Golconda are mutually exclusive mystical states. That you cannot follow one path without abandoning the other. This very well may be true. If it is, some Osirans may doggedly persist in believing they can have their cake and eat it too. Characters are allowed to be mistaken. Others, by contrast, may be aware of this possibility, and either accept that they won't be able to achieve Golconda, or work to follow it <i>by proxy</i>.<br /> <br /> There are two possibilities in the latter case. One, the Osiran works almost as a vampiric Bodhisattva, not pursuing their own transcendence in order to help other vampires seek Golconda for its own sake. Two, the Osiran uses other characters as a test case, following their progress with the hopes that they can follow the path themselves later, once they have a better idea of how it is done. The former is hardly sinister at all, while the latter is only potentially manipulative, since the Osiran is throwing someone else into the spiritual lion's den in hopes of riding their coattails. A given character may be motivated by both.<br /> <br /> The above scenario may give reason as to why an Osiran NPC might avoid teaching a PC Bardo. They need the character to be solidly OFF the path of Osiris, if they're to follow Golconda unimpeded. Whether the NPC is open about this, or is not forthcoming about it, depends on their motivations and the role the ST wants for them. If they're intended to be an honest force, they may give the PC the choice of learning Bardo, with the understanding they may never achieve Golconda if they do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 07-19-2021, 01:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T00:21:04-07:00","id":1455177,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455170#post1455170\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is, of course, the very real possibility that Bardo and Golconda are mutually exclusive mystical states. That you cannot follow one path without abandoning the other. This very well may be true. If it is, some Osirans may doggedly persist in believing they can have their cake and eat it too. Characters are allowed to be mistaken. Others, by contrast, may be aware of this possibility, and either accept that they won't be able to achieve Golconda, or work to follow it <i>by proxy</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>One nice thing about this is that it restores a sense of mystery to a chronicle. What is the relationship between Bardo and Golconda, if any? Or the teachings of Osiris (which obviously includes Bardo but shouldn't be limited to that) and Golconda? Even when the ST has a firm answer in mind, within the setting itself, the NPCs shouldn't know definitively. I think any NPC who is a member of the Children of Osiris believes Osiris' doctrine is correct, but those NPCs can still disagree as to how encompassing that doctrine is. Is it an alternative to Golconda? A supplement? Are they right and Golconda seekers objectively wrong? And those NPCs who know of the Children and Bardo, but aren't members can have even more perspectives.<br /> <br /> A negative result of the game eventually publishing so many sourcebooks providing canonical answers to things is that it robbed the setting of a sense of mystery. Even if a PC didn't know something, a well-read player likely did, and as much as they may not want to metagame, they can't help knowing the answer to a lot of things which should be mysteries in the game. It's one reason I like to view supplements as a tool kit, or as EXAMPLES of what might be true rather than a literary canon that explains everything. I think a good ST has to diverge from a lot of canon in order to restore mystery to the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455170#post1455170\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are two possibilities in the latter case. One, the Osiran works almost as a vampiric Bodhisattva, not pursuing their own transcendence in order to help other vampires seek Golconda for its own sake. Two, the Osiran uses other characters as a test case, following their progress with the hopes that they can follow the path themselves later, once they have a better idea of how it is done. The former is hardly sinister at all, while the latter is only potentially manipulative, since the Osiran is throwing someone else into the spiritual lion's den in hopes of riding their coattails. A given character may be motivated by both.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an interesting take and one I had not considered. It makes at least some of the Children manipulative and not entirely altruistic, which is a good corrective to the idea that they are white hat good guys. But it still seems true to the concept unlike making them hypocritical, brainwashed assholes. It also exposes some diversity within the Children which is always good with any group.<br /> <br /> I really like your ideas here. At the beginning of this thread, even though I had some idea of how to use the Osirans, it was still rather one-note. Now for the first time, I am truly seeing the potentials in them as a sect with diverse members.<br /> <br /> Another thing I think that should be taken seriously is the perspective IC of how amazing Bardo and the accomplishments of Osiris are in the context of vampires. If you are a vampire really concerned about Humanity, Bardo is like a miracle. Being told one guy developed all of this could be profound. And as you said, unlike Golconda, there is considerable proof Bardo works. It makes me think that within the setting, there should be much more discussion about Bardo and the Children of Osiris than there typically is. At least as much as there is discussion about Golconda. And to be honest, at the perspective of your average Neonate, there probably shouldn't be much distinction between the concepts of Golconda and Bardo/Osirans. A lot of it just merges into a combined superstition. <i>&quot;Hey, dude, if you are interested in Golconda, seek out the Children of Osiris. They know.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> This thread has helped me determine a much better portrayal and potential use of the Children of Osiris than I had coming into it. A good example of how discussion of a topic can improve one's understanding or thinking. I still don't know if I'll ever use the Children of Osiris in a chronicle, but at least I know better how I want to present them in my chronicles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T02:08:33-07:00","id":1455179,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455177#post1455177\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A negative result of the game eventually publishing so many sourcebooks providing canonical answers to things is that it robbed the setting of a sense of mystery. Even if a PC didn't know something, a well-read player likely did, and as much as they may not want to metagame, they can't help knowing the answer to a lot of things which should be mysteries in the game. It's one reason I like to view supplements as a tool kit, or as EXAMPLES of what might be true rather than a literary canon that explains everything. I think a good ST has to diverge from a lot of canon in order to restore mystery to the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ironically one of the advantages of the Osirans being low-key &quot;Squatted&quot; before they got any traction is that they're really mysterious, even to many Vampire fans. And because there's not a lot of material to begin with, the ST is free to make up whatever they want.<br /> <br /> The downside, of course, being that there's little material to work with, and thus little direction in how to really make use of the Children.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Another thing I think that should be taken seriously is the perspective IC of how amazing Bardo and the accomplishments of Osiris are in the context of vampires. If you are a vampire really concerned about Humanity, Bardo is like a miracle. Being told one guy developed all of this could be profound. And as you said, unlike Golconda, there is considerable proof Bardo works. It makes me think that within the setting, there should be much more discussion about Bardo and the Children of Osiris than there typically is. At least as much as there is discussion about Golconda. And to be honest, at the perspective of your average Neonate, there probably shouldn't be much distinction between the concepts of Golconda and Bardo/Osirans. A lot of it just merges into a combined superstition. <i>&quot;Hey, dude, if you are interested in Golconda, seek out the Children of Osiris. They know.&quot;</i> </div> </div> </div>I imagine the Children of Osiris probably would be the talk of the vampiric world, if they weren't super secretive by necessity.<br /> <br /> Which is a shame, since it would be the answer to many vampires's prayers. On the other hand, there are probably loads of vampires who not only wouldn't care to adopt the Osiran unlifestyle, but would consider the existence of such a philosophy and Discipline to be contrary to their interests within the Jyhad. Especially if it somehow became a widespread thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This thread has helped me determine a much better portrayal and potential use of the Children of Osiris than I had coming into it. A good example of how discussion of a topic can improve one's understanding or thinking. I still don't know if I'll ever use the Children of Osiris in a chronicle, but at least I know better how I want to present them in my chronicles. </div> </div> </div>One of the greatest missed opportunities to come from how the metaplot of VtM developed over the years was the removal or sidelining of elements like the Children of Osiris. Because the Children didn't fit into the dark, cynical direction the game was going in, they got the axe. If not being outright destroyed, then having all their vampires excised, and left as more of a vestigial element within the lore (such as in Sorcerer or Mummy).<br /> <br /> We never got to see the faction developed, and thus we never really got to have these discussions.<br /> <br /> This is why I hold to the idea that, all else being equal, more options is better than fewer. We've had Egyptian themed factions, and we've had &quot;nice guy&quot; factions. But nothing in VtM or the wider World of Darkness is quite like the Children of Osiris.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T02:35:12-07:00","id":1455180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455179#post1455179\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is why I hold to the idea that, all else being equal, more options is better than fewer. We've had Egyptian themed factions, and we've had &quot;nice guy&quot; factions. But nothing in VtM or the wider World of Darkness is quite like the Children of Osiris.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really, too many extranious options tend to detract from themes. The Camarilla, for instance, worked because it had a monopoly. Granted, there are lots of options for how to play the Camarilla (Tyranical, hands-off, a necessary evil, conservative, modern and enlightened) but it primarily works because your other options are either extreme cults or whatever the fuck the anarchs are failing to do. Once you provide valid alternatives the game loses what makes the Camarilla great. Part of the wonder of Vampire is the pressure you're under to conform to vampire standards. You are a piece of coal and the constraints your under are turning you into a diamond. Factions like the Tremere/Sabbat/Assamites with their -Conform or die- attitude just make the world so interesting. Without that pressure, the hose is weak. The availability of factions that can give players a good &quot;way out&quot; like the Children of Osiris really detracts from the pressure of the Jyhad. The inconnu only survive, albiet further pushed to the margins, because their standards for joining are way too high for players. For the CoO to work, they need to provide a similar level of pressure on the players; Perhaps their dogma needs to be hard, or they need to be corrupt, or they need to be horrendously persecuted. You can't just have a nice secret society that'll start paying attention to players with humanity 8+","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T11:36:18-07:00","id":1455205,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455179#post1455179\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I imagine the Children of Osiris probably would be the talk of the vampiric world, if they weren't super secretive by necessity.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is probably a right level of mystery for them as well as any other concept or faction in the WoD. As originally presented, concepts like Golconda and Diabelrie were meant to be almost equally unheard of mysteries. In 1e Chicago By Night, very prominent vampires decades old had only just begun to learn about them and did not know any details (Sovereign in regards to Diablerie, and Capone to Golconda). And while there were older vampires who knew more, I always assumed that the vast majority of the vampires in Chicago were simply ignorant. Yet the <i>players</i> obviously knew of this because they were written in the main RPG sourcebook which we had all read. As a result of this player knowledge, many players just assumed that characters were equally informed and the setting eventually shifted to make that assumption reality. But I like the idea that certain things are just only known as rumor, and unless you are really interested in that subject, that's all it becomes. If you're not, then it's just a fringe theory. <br /> <br /> (And now that I think about it, any chronicle should probably have a list of false rumors, groups, factions, etc. that fill similar role that only serve as red herrings. That way you can have an NPC say stuff like &quot;I know the rumors, but groups like the Children of Osiris, the Brotherhood of Enoch, and the Disciples of Zagreus don't exist. And if they ever did, any knowledge they had died out long ago.&quot; Hopefully that will get the PCs into thinking these fictional groups do exist in your chronicle. And if they don't, that's why an ST should also have new factions that only exist in their chronicles so they'll be shocked when they learn there is such a thing called the Brotherhood of Enoch. Players <i>know</i> the setting too well for there to truly be a sense of mystery now. Too many secrets revealed. This is why STs should change things up while keeping the basic core of the setting intact.)<br /> <br /> And that's how I feel knowledge about the Children of Osiris should likely be. At a certain point in their unlife, a vampire hears the name and a vague rumor associated with them. And that's it. Nothing about Bardo and certainly no specifics. Just the idea of &quot;hey, if you want to retain/restore your humanity (or become mortal again), it is said the Children of Osiris know the secret.&quot; Just enough that such rumors can be easily dismissed as hogwash, or that the Children have long since been destroyed or never existed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455179#post1455179\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the greatest missed opportunities to come from how the metaplot of VtM developed over the years was the removal or sidelining of elements like the Children of Osiris. Because the Children didn't fit into the dark, cynical direction the game was going in, they got the axe. If not being outright destroyed, then having all their vampires excised, and left as more of a vestigial element within the lore (such as in Sorcerer or Mummy).<br /> <br /> We never got to see the faction developed, and thus we never really got to have these discussions.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is now why I see the Revised setting as not in continuity with the early version, but essentially an &quot;Earth-1 and Earth-2&quot; scenario. They're two different versions of Vampire that simply kept a lot of the same names, sometimes the same characters, but used them all differently. V5 is basically an Earth-3 version and likewise has no continuity between alternate versions. There's nothing wrong with an ST doing that - it's what we all do. But the carelessness by which the Developers discard, change, or even retcon things damages the integrity of the setting. V20 works well as an agnostic setting allowing STs to treat it like a salad bar.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455179#post1455179\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is why I hold to the idea that, all else being equal, more options is better than fewer.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like to view the sourcebooks as being toolkits - what can I utilize out of them to tell the stories <b>I</b> want to tell? As a result, I like lots of options. <br /> <br /> But in terms of the setting itself, the sheer number of groups in canon is just ridiculous. Way too many bloodlines. Way too many supernatural creatures. Groups originally meant to be small and few in number explode in population because people (naturally) like them and want to use them in game. World becomes way too crowded. So in my chronicles, I end up hacking them away. Unless there is something I find particularly interesting about a particular minor faction, they just don't exist in my chronicles. No sub-clans like the Lamia or Anda or Assamite Sorcerers. No Ahrimanes, Daughters of Cacophony, Kiasyd, or True Brujah. And lots of other things get the axe. And even those things that I do keep in my version of the setting (like the Samedi), I don't use them in a chronicle unless I specifically have an important chronicle reason in mind.<br /> <br /> What gets kept and what gets the axe is purely a matter of taste. If others used the same method of disposing what doesn't interest them and keeping what they like, they'll make different decisions. They might get far greater mileage out of the Ahrimanes than I ever could.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T20:55:10-07:00","id":1455233,"title":"","content":"I admit, if I were rewriting them, I'd go JUSTIN ACHILLI on it and go dark.<br /> ----<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Children of Osiris Revised</b><br /> <br /> <b>Nickname</b>: Cultists (vulgar), Osirians (formal)<br /> <br /> <b>Disciplines</b>: Animalism, Blood Sorcery (Bardo), Presence<br /> <br /> <b>Description</b>: The Children of Osiris are a group that are known about among Kindred and usually derided or existing on the fringes of vampire society. They take in Caitiff, Thin Bloods, and those on the run from the Camarilla or Anarchs.<br /> <br /> Those who join the Children of Osiris are very rarely ever seen again and this contributes to people choosing not to seek them out save as a last resort. Most Kindred believe, correctly, the Osirians are a religious cult that will not let you leave if you join them. That is, if you are allowed to survive and join their ranks in the first place. The Osirians are, in simple terms, a Humanities cult.<br /> <br /> They revere the &quot;concept&quot; of Humanity and despise being a vampire like many other Blood Cults but they take the concept in a bizarre direction. Its members practice monasticism, self-denial, flagellation, and drinking from one another as well as animals at the higher generations to avoid ever harming humans. They also drink from their fellow Kindred that they hunt.<br /> <br /> The Children of Osiris have exacting standards on what they consider to be proper etiquette among the undead. Only those willing to completely empty their minds and agree to become members of the cult according to their beliefs are allowed to become full members. The rest are staked then fed until they can exact enough blood to feed the group for some time until they leave them for the sun as sacrifices.<br /> <br /> As such, the Camarilla considers the Children of Osiris to be a hostile degenerate cult of cannibals even though they don't officially practice diablerie. Surprisingly, they have avoided complete destruction by the Camarilla due to the fact that many Princes consider what they do to be a service that leads away the weak minded of their domains. The Children of Osiris are never stupid enough to actually attempt to overthrow the power structures of vampirekind but exist as an &quot;alternative&quot; on the fringes of more popular domains. The Anarchs and Sabbat are not so forgiving with many leading overt attacks on their group havens, often with great casualties ensuing. The Children of Osiris are strong from the blood they drink, freely share disciplines, and often possess fanatical loyalty to each other.<br /> <br /> The Osirians practice their own Egyptian-flavored form of Blood Magic called Bardo that provides them rituals to regain lost Humanity as well as make animal blood more palatable. Those who observe these rituals wonder if the Osirians are actually brainwashing their followers while mentally binding them to specific behaviors. Others believe that their magic is a boon to Kindred society and wish it was more freely available.<br /> <br /> There is some suggestion that the Children of Osiris are a creation of the Red List member, Kemintiri. The ancient may have created the cult and their magic as a way to undermine the rest of Kindred society. If so, it has not yet been proven and she may have abandoned her weapon long ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-20T07:33:25-07:00","id":1455257,"title":"","content":"I prefer them as originally envisioned, as someone who thinks Revised was way off track. The world that is all grim dark isn't really a world, more like just one big cliche. More Buddhist than Cult. This thread has convinced me to use them, and am already writing up a story to slide them into. First Govanni, now Children of Osiris. What is this board going to convince me to use next?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-20T11:27:52-07:00","id":1455267,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vamps Like Us</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455257#post1455257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I prefer them as originally envisioned, as someone who thinks Revised was way off track. The world that is all grim dark isn't really a world, more like just one big cliche. More Buddhist than Cult. This thread has convinced me to use them, and am already writing up a story to slide them into. First Govanni, now Children of Osiris. What is this board going to convince me to use next?</div> </div> </div> </div>Same. Anything Phipps despises that much as written is probably something that belongs in my campaign... because his preferences are invariably bleak and depressing from my perspective. I'm more of a &quot;you can't have shadows without some light&quot; type of GM myself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-20T12:06:22-07:00","id":1455271,"title":"","content":"I mostly wonder how many vampires meet the criteria of being humane enough to attract the CoO whilst also being politically savvy enough to survive long enough to join. They must rely upon older vampires looking to change (who could be settite plants) or be strong advocates of happenstance. Poaching childer from active participants of the Jyhad is not going to go swimmingly, especially in the case that the CoO are reluctant to get their hands dirty. I think there's definetly going to be some wholesome true believers among them, any religion has good folks and bad folks among them, but ultimately the more humane the CoO, the less feasible their logistics as an underground and heavily persecuted cult.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-20T14:32:22-07:00","id":1455279,"title":"","content":"Hmm. I enjoy my hard-earned high humanity vampires as much as my jaded assholes and enlightened path followers. <br /> <br /> And yet I don't like the Osirians. I also don't like considering the Salubri to be a force for good, and would rather they both be heavily flawed and/or hypocritical in some fashion. In the CoO's case, I think it might be due to Bardo. It feels... artificial, maybe? I dunno. I suppose it's like: &quot;Vampire support groups, yes. Magical cop outs from dealing with the same shit as other vampires, no.&quot;<br /> <br /> I would have them be just that: a cop out. A way for vampires to try and cheat at humanity, only to find themselves dependent on Bardo and their new cult and no longer able to manage the beast and humanity without it.<br /> <br /> I like my bright spots to be in the main factions and 13 clans, by vampires like everyone else. It's a hard road that can be taken by anyone, no special bloodlines or powers, just the will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 07-20-2021, 06:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T12:28:59-07:00","id":1455320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris#post1450257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do the Children of Osiris add anything to V:TM or are they redundant with the Salubri?</div> </div> </div> </div>Simple answer: No, but yes.<br /> <br /> As a bloodline, and barely a bloodline at that, they are worse than redundant; they are problematic as a bloodline you're not embraced into, but one you just join up? How is that even a thing?<br /> <br /> But a better use of them would be not as a bloodline, but as an example of a highly specialized vampire cult. I mean, you still have your clan flaw, still have two of your disciplines, so you're effectively still a member of your old clan. But as a starting concept for specialized blood cults, they open up a design space where you join some fanatical order for access to some highly specialized discipline. You're still a member of your clan, but you're also a part of the Cult of Set-Beaters so you can access the highly specialized skills of Humanity-Protection and Set-Beating.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writing up Clanbook: Aabbt","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/700-vysha","name":"Vysha","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar700_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"602"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T13:52:22-07:00","id":1455324,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vysha</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455320#post1455320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Simple answer: No, but yes.<br /> <br /> As a bloodline, and barely a bloodline at that, they are worse than redundant; they are problematic as a bloodline you're not embraced into, but one you just join up? How is that even a thing?<br /> <br /> But a better use of them would be not as a bloodline, but as an example of a highly specialized vampire cult. I mean, you still have your clan flaw, still have two of your disciplines, so you're effectively still a member of your old clan. But as a starting concept for specialized blood cults, they open up a design space where you join some fanatical order for access to some highly specialized discipline. You're still a member of your clan, but you're also a part of the Cult of Set-Beaters so you can access the highly specialized skills of Humanity-Protection and Set-Beating.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. They're what would be called a Blood Cult rather than a bloodline. Sort of like why I find it weird the Hecata had to magically merge versus being a micro-sect.<br /> <br /> Mind you, I don't like to make everything grimdark. I just prefer the PCs to be more likely to be the flawless paragons, if they choose to be such, versus other vampires.<br /> <br /> I'm a big fan of noir influences and think the detective genre would lose something if there were other heroes than the protagonists. The police should always be corrupt and institutions.<br /> <br /> <i>\u201cdown these mean streets a man must go who is not himself mean, who is neither tarnished nor afraid. He is the hero; he is everything. He must be a complete man and a common man and yet an unusual man. He must be, to use a rather weathered phrase, a man of honor\u2014by instinct, by inevitability, without thought of it, and certainly without saying it. He must be the best man in his world and a good enough man for any world.&quot;</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 07-21-2021, 05:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T15:13:44-07:00","id":1455328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455271#post1455271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mostly wonder how many vampires meet the criteria of being humane enough to attract the CoO whilst also being politically savvy enough to survive long enough to join.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see the Children as actively recruiting. In fact, given their need for secrecy, the risks far outweigh any gains. Simply because someone has high levels of humanity does not mean they have any desire to join the Children, nor do the Children have any desire for them to join. This is an order of ascetic monk vampires in discipleship to the Great Undying King. They already know most vampires aren't interested in this (and let's be honest, probably no PCs. The Children are really just an NPC faction in the setting).<br /> <br /> Insofar that the Children add members to their sect, it is because vampires learn about them independently, pursued the (or at least their teachings), and at some point come to the attention of the Children who evaluate them as candidates. If the candidate is rejected, the Children would remain hidden. If the candidate is considered acceptable, then an existing Children would make contact and begin the process of discipleship. And only after a period of work and evaluation, would that vampire ever be made an offer to actually join. The Children need to see the candidate essentially performing a kind of spiritual pilgrimage. Some amount of spiritual growth needs to be seen for the Children to actively provide them with any information, contact, and eventually initiation into their order.<br /> <br /> Another potential source of contact is when the Children are trying to destroy a particular vampire, and in the course of their operations meet others who can further their aims. However, I think this is much more likely to be done via a kind of spycraft, and the Children never identify themselves as Osirans. They'll use a plausible enough cover story. Then they disappear. Certain vampires they interacted with may intrigue them, but they likely only go on a kind of watch list.<br /> <br /> Now new members do join the Children at some point. But these must be a very small number over a long time period. The Osirans aren't trying to build an army. They are an order of ascetic vampires trying to negate their Curse/Damnation while fighting a clandestine war against those trying to destroy them. Their primary issue should be to preserve the teachings of Osiris and secondly to destroy their enemies (most likely when commanded by the Grand Undying King). Recruitment is likely down on their list of priorities.<br /> <br /> So for the perspective of how do the Children recruit, I think a better route would be &quot;how do the Children disseminate the teachings of Osiris?&quot;<br /> <br /> I think this is when we talk about front organizations and the like. In mortal culture, this could be through Egyptological societies and studies, pseudo-Eastern/New Age mysticism, maybe even self-help groups, therapists, and the like. Since this is directed towards mortals and not vampires, there's not a lot here that is useful to vampires. But it has the purposes of 1) getting certain ideas in the public consciousness that can make their way to vampires, and 2) building some kind of actual &quot;real&quot; (WoD) occult group behind it that might attract the kind of vampire they are looking for. This could suggest there is some kind of obscure &quot;school&quot; (I think any real organization would be too hierarchical, structured, centralized for the Children's purpose) of Egyptian style Sorcery, most of whose &quot;members&quot; are likely ignorant. But there may be a few trusted mortals who know a lot more about the Children (who might appear as just truly aware magicians, or some kind of supernatural (perhaps divine) entity but not vampires) and can be entrusted to carry out orders on their behalf.<br /> <br /> In terms of vampires, the Children need to be much more secretive and circumspect. In so far that they run &quot;black ops&quot; style hits on their enemies, they might leave certain messages or clues among anyone who helped them that serve the eventual purpose of getting the word out. And then leave it up them to spread any rumors. And perhaps over time, they may even recruit certain agents (useful and trusted allies who nevertheless are rejected as future members) to disseminate other information for them. This would be a combination of disinformation and propaganda selectively spread over a long period of time.<br /> <br /> That would establish a certain body of lore collectively known among the greater vampire community. Then as vampires self-select themselves to follow these teachings or learn more about them, it gives the Children a certain amount of time to observe and evaluate them. And instead of leading a vampire directly to the Children, it just leads to a variety of front organizations (which might be actual Buddhist temples, Christian monasteries, or whatever) that can reveal just enough to the vampire seeker that they are on the right trail, to keep them going, while giving an opportunity to the Children that a candidate is still looking for their teachings. That way if it is one of their enemies attempting to infiltrate them, at most they lose a few unwitting pawns who may know of the Children only though allusions, euphemisms, or legends but can't possibly provide any useful information to their enemies.<br /> <br /> Kind of like how both Dr Strange and Dr Doom pursued the Himalayan monks/hermits that taught them secrets of the mystic arts in their comic book origins. They pursue one rumor to another. STs wanting to do this for one of the PCs in their chronicle, probably needs to run it as a kind of Dan Brown style occult mystery/conspiracy where the PC begins to figure out enigmas and clues that gets them closer and closer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 11-26-2021, 09:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T15:34:21-07:00","id":1455329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455324#post1455324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a big fan of noir influences and think the detective genre would lose something if there were other heroes than the protagonists. The police should always be corrupt and institutions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Most noir/hard boiled/detective stories have allies and friends of the hero that he can rely on. Batman has Commissioner Gordon. Spenser has Hawk. Mike Hammer's best friend is a captain of homicide for the NYPD. Many detectives have that &quot;one old friend&quot; on the force who is honest. Even police officials who complain about the detective or harass them often come to their aid (Inspector Lestrange for Holmes, Inspector Cramer for Nero Wolfe &amp; Archie Goodwin). The Shadow has a variety of agents who support him.<br /> <br /> Some of these allies are weaker than the protagonist and others are more powerful. Some are worse morally and others better. But they are just secondary to the specific <i>story</i> being told regardless of their position in the <i>setting</i>.<br /> <br /> I think very few noir or hardboiled heroes actually have <i>no one</i> who helps them, and they are all on their own. Maybe the Continental Op, but even he has an entire detective organization behind him theoretically.<br /> <br /> So I think it is perfectly fine in the WoD setting for there to be the Children of Osiris and still keep that gritty noir feel. It's just that they should never usurp the attention of the ST/chronicle away from the NPCs or solve the problem for them so that the PCs are superfluous. (Too bad there's a lot of awful Vampire adventures out there that did just that - just not with any Children of Osiris.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T20:09:40-07:00","id":1455341,"title":"","content":"I find the Children of Osiris work pretty well as a sort of mysterious side group that can sometimes help or hinder the PCs. They're also great when I'm running a game of Mummy or Werewolf of whatever and need a group of friendly(er) vampires to serve as assistances or aids to the player characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-22T22:06:49-07:00","id":1455415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455329#post1455329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most noir/hard boiled/detective stories have allies and friends of the hero that he can rely on. Batman has Commissioner Gordon. Spenser has Hawk. Mike Hammer's best friend is a captain of homicide for the NYPD. Many detectives have that &quot;one old friend&quot; on the force who is honest. Even police officials who complain about the detective or harass them often come to their aid (Inspector Lestrange for Holmes, Inspector Cramer for Nero Wolfe &amp; Archie Goodwin). The Shadow has a variety of agents who support him.<br /> <br /> Some of these allies are weaker than the protagonist and others are more powerful. Some are worse morally and others better. But they are just secondary to the specific <i>story</i> being told regardless of their position in the <i>setting</i>.<br /> <br /> I think very few noir or hardboiled heroes actually have <i>no one</i> who helps them, and they are all on their own. Maybe the Continental Op, but even he has an entire detective organization behind him theoretically.<br /> <br /> So I think it is perfectly fine in the WoD setting for there to be the Children of Osiris and still keep that gritty noir feel. It's just that they should never usurp the attention of the ST/chronicle away from the NPCs or solve the problem for them so that the PCs are superfluous. (Too bad there's a lot of awful Vampire adventures out there that did just that - just not with any Children of Osiris.)</div> </div> </div> </div>We could take a page from D&amp;D's book, and have NPCs that might be more powerful than the PCs, but consequently have a lot more on their plate. They see things from an elevated perspective, and so can't be everywhere at once. Or else, as is the case with the Osirans, they're organizationally constrained, and thus can't always deal with things themselves without massively exposing their presence. In either case, they delegate. Pointing PCs towards tasks that need doing, to free themselves up to tackle matters more pressing elsewhere.<br /> <br /> In the Forgotten Realms, the Masked Lords of Waterdeep can't deal with a zombie infestation, because they're literally lords of a city, with jobs to do. For the Osirans, they're having to keep tabs on a bunch of areas in the Jyhad - not least of which the Setites - AND maintain their personal unlifestyle of asceticism and meditation. Inevitably, they're going to run up against walls they cannot or will not handle themselves.<br /> <br /> There's pragmatic reasons for acting as &quot;quest giver&quot;, of course. For one, it separates the wheat from the chaff, allowing the Osirans to gauge a PC's potential as a recruit...or as a threat. All depending on how the characters decide to handle situations. For another, it keeps pesky fledglings and neonates busy, in a &quot;productive&quot; manner. Idle hands are the devil's playthings, or so they say, and giving young vampires work to do possibly keeps them from engaging in &quot;mischief&quot;. (Which is very much not to say the PCs aren't capable of doing both at the same time. Players have a boundless capacity to both solve problems and exploit them for fun and profit.)<br /> <br /> And if a PC turns out to be really rotten - just an all-around terrible and dangerous lick - leading them into hazardous situations can get them killed. Either the vampire solves the problem or dies trying. Win win. (Obviously, a high Humanity vampire is unlikely to reserve this kind of callous treatment for ones that prove they can't be trusted or that arguably &quot;need to die&quot;. Otherwise, this is just standard Elder behavior.)<br /> <br /> In any event, the Osiran, by necessity, will engage in &quot;quest giving&quot; through intermediaries or covert actions. The PCs may first become aware of their benefactor through drops or secret messages, pointing them towards unsavory dealings, mysteries, or information relevant to their interests. It may be many stories along through the chronicle before meeting the Osiran and learning their nature is even an option.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T02:56:39-07:00","id":1455426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455415#post1455415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's pragmatic reasons for acting as &quot;quest giver&quot; of course.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also by giving suggestions or advice to someone else it can help keep your Beast from getting to strong. Your catspaw does the dirty work, loses Humanity, but you get to keep yours since you were &quot;just asking questions&quot; or &quot;giving advice.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T13:06:12-07:00","id":1455458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455426#post1455426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also by giving suggestions or advice to someone else it can help keep your Beast from getting to strong. Your catspaw does the dirty work, loses Humanity, but you get to keep yours since you were &quot;just asking questions&quot; or &quot;giving advice.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;I mean, it's not my fault they took my warning, and resolved it in the most morally bankrupt way possible. I didn't ask them to burn down that orphanage.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T22:28:28-07:00","id":1455479,"title":"","content":"Whether or not the Children of Osiris are to your taste, I think the discussion has been good at determing the type of group that they are. So now I think is a good time to step back and look at the Salubri again, so we can compare the two.<br /> <br /> For this post, I am mostly going by the original description of the Salubri in the early Player Guides. Their depiction in Dark Ages and Revised, while derived from them, inevitably introduced things that caused some divergences or neglected aspects I think are important. Furthermore, I also think it is important to view the Salubri through the eyes of what is known by the NPCs of Vampire in general, and not the \u201csecret knowledge\u201d we have as players/readers of the sourcebooks. So here are the major points of the bloodline in the modern era that I think are most important to remember.<ul><li>The Salubri are very few with official numbers being only seven, although I think that at any one time their numbers may be higher or lower than that given that each one is supposed to sire, and they are being hunted and subject to destruction. (Whether or not their numbers being \u201cseven\u201d is important to the bloodline are unknown, but it is important to remember that seven is a magical number.) Do their numbers have to be seven? At some point was it ten until enough were destroyed that there is now only seven? If some of them are subsequently destroyed, would the number now be only five exist? Or somehow has their numbers been consistently seven on purpose? We don't know although the way it is written in the Players Guide (\u201cOnly seven of these Cainites exist at a time.\u201d) sounds like this is a set number. But if so, how do they replace their numbers if one of the mentors gets destroyed?</li> <li>The Salubri are well known to modern vampires, likely as a result of Tremere propaganda. Furthermore, they are not just a memory \u2013 they are known to exist given that some Princes have called blood hunts on them. They also have a very bad reputation being considered to be diablerists, infernalists, and \u201csoulsuckers\u201d.</li> <li>Salubri are obsessed with Golconda not just for vampires, but also for mortals. To Salubri, Golconda isn't just a vampire thing, but something that is important to mortals as well. If you don't achieve Golconda, you are doomed to become a \u201cbound spirit.\u201d In general, they are very interested in the state of people's \u201csouls\u201d. From this description, it's unclear whether the Salubri believe Golconda is exactly the same thing as described in the books, or encompasses some greater aspect so that it involves mortals.</li> <li>Salubri always identify a mortal to embrace and then teach them how to achieve Golconda. Then when the Salubri mentor actually achieves Golconda, they command their childe to diablerize them. To me, this is a very strange thing to do for a vampire in Golconda. Why embrace at all? I think we as players assume that most vampires who have reached Golconda won't embrace. But the Salubri always do this. We've just been told that even mortals need to reach Golconda. But if they can, why embrace them? Do they need to first become vampires to reach Golconda? If so, why only limit it to one person, dooming most humans? Furthermore, why is it essential for the Salubri to diablerize their sire? This requirement is never explained.</li> <li>Another question is if this is the current culture of the Salubri, exactly when did this begin? Why are all Salubri the exact same generation (8th \u2013 although obiously their children must be 9th until they diablerize their sires)? How come in the past 500-1000 years, none of the Salubri embraced ever deviated from it? Might it be a result of the diablerie influencing the newest Salubri, meaning they are somehow being controlled by past Salubri? Is it just a coincidence that exactly seven members of the same generation has been able to survive? They are some very strange things here without explanation, and it's no wonder a lot of players were suspicious about the Salubri. It is very easy for someone to take the elements of the above and construct a very different reason why the Salubri act the way they do.</li> <li>Another thing we're told (in the characetr creation section) is that they often have large Herds - \u201cfaithful followers\u201d who allow the Salubri to feed on them (avoiding the Salubri clan weakness). This means most, if not all, Salubri are basically leaders of a blood cult. And it likely means that anytime a Salubri embraces a student/replacement, the person they choose is from their Herd (whom they are likely teaching about Golconda and their ideas about souls). In fact, we're explicitly told that the Salubri consider it a duty to explain their concepts to everyone they meet.</li> <li>A corollary of character creation is that all Salubri must be relatively young in vampire terms. Some may only be a few years or decades old. Even the eldest is probably only a few centuries old as opposed to an ancient elder. The constant diablerie must keep the Salubri refreshed and relatively young.</li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T22:33:11-07:00","id":1455480,"title":"","content":"Having reviewed the Salubri, we can now compare them to the Children of Osiris, and doing so we will notice a lot of differences.<br /> <br /> <b>Clan versus Sect</b><br /> The Salubri are an extremely small bloodline with a common founder, Saulot. The Children are not related by blood, just by acceptance of the philosophy of Osiris and the rule of the Grand Undying King. So Kindred of many clans are members in it. (This last bit is something often overlooked in editions after first by the misconception that since the Children have a common Discipline, this means they must be a bloodline.)<br /> <br /> <b>Few versus Many</b><br /> The Salubri are much smaller in number than the Children of Osiris. There are only supposed to be seven at one time (although when we combine both student and mentor, there can be more). In contrast, while still a small group, the Children seem like they have more members \u2013 maybe a few dozen although we're not told outright. Whatever the ST decides for their chronicle though, the Children should outnumber the Salubri by a significant difference I would think.<br /> <br /> <b>Informal versus an Order</b><br /> The organization of the two are very different. Salubri generally live alone and interact rarely with other Salubri. Except for the mentor/student relationship, Salubri do not hang out with one another. In contrast, the Children appear to be an ascetic order ruled by some kind of monastic rule governed by the Grand Undying King. This does not mean the Children have to live among one another, but they definitely appear much more organized than the Salubri and likely have to obey the Grand Undying King as the leader of their group.<br /> <br /> <b>Salubri are Young while the Children are Old</b><br /> Because of constant diablerie, the Salubri should not be very old by vampire standards. In contrast, some of the Children of Osiris are incredibly old. The Grand Undying King was embraced by Osiris himself, making him a Methusaleh thousands of years old.<br /> <br /> <b>Salubri Embrace but the Children Don't</b><br /> Salubri seem to believe that Golconda is so important to achieve, they MUST embrace someone in order to pass it on. Examples of the people embraced include actual <i>children</i>. In contrast, the Children of Osiris refuse to embrace, seeking only to teach existing vampires their philosophy. I can't see them ever embracing a child or thinking that is an OK thing to do.<br /> <br /> <b>Salubri usually feed on human Herds, but the Children try to avoid feeding on humans at all</b><br /> This is another important distinction. The Children of Osiris make an effort to avoid feeding on human blood. They clearly prefer to feed on animals. They do this even though they don't have a weakness that causes problems if they feed on humans. In contrast despite having a clan weakness that involves feeding on unwilling victims, the Salubri in general decided on a solution on creating large Herds of humans around them as a blood cult.<br /> <br /> <b>Salubri Practice Diablerie while the Children Do Not</b><br /> This is another important distinction. Salubri practice ritualized diablerie. I can't imagine the Children of Osiris ever justifying diablerie. Furthremore, it appears that the young Salubri may not even get a choice, as the text claims \u201c[They] commit suicide by <i>forcing</i> the Childe to drink her blood.\u201d How are they forced? We don't know. Dominate? Indoctrination? Something else? It's problematic.<br /> <br /> <b>Known versus Unknown</b><br /> Salubri are known, or at least believed, to exist. In contrast, almost everyone thinks the Children of Osiris don't exist having perished millenia ago.<br /> <br /> <b>Feared &amp; Hated versus Indifference</b><br /> Salubri have terrible reputations and actively hunted by almost everyone. In contrast, nobody cares about the Children of Osiris (except maybe the Setites). It's mostly because people don't think they exist anymore because also because the Children aren't said to do the same things they Salubri do (diablerize, run blood cults of brainwashed humans, remove souls from people). And those are just the things we know they actually do. Then there are the rumors that may be objectively untrue (infernalism) but given the odd actions that require some explanation not provided in the text, it's not inconceivable.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunted versus Hunter</b><br /> As Elphilim mentioned, the Salubri are being actively hunted everyone for various reasons. In contrast, while the Children of Osiris do have enemies, they are not being actively persecuted even by the Followers of Set since most people think they haven't existed for centuries. Nor does anyone outside these enemies would have any reason to do so. The Children of Osiris don't engage in any of the strange behavior of the Salubri that causes such offense.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T22:35:36-07:00","id":1455481,"title":"","content":"I'll admit that before this thread, I didn't give all much thought to either the Salubri or Children of Osiris. Much less compare the two for differences. I just had some vague ideas based on memories of when I originally read about them. But now that I have, I would never portray the two groups as similar or run an NPC of one group like I would to another.<br /> <br /> To my eyes, the Children are clearly the more sympathetic faction despite not pushing Golconda and preferring instead Bardo. In terms of behavior, for the most part the Children of Osiris act much more humane and avoid scandalous behavior. In contrast, the Salubri engage in a lot of questionable practices, and it makes a lot of sense why other vampires don't trust them. The Children appear to be ascetic monks that don't force their opinions on people. The Salubri appear to be a deranged group of religious/cult fanatics with lots of frieghtening behavior.<br /> <br /> A big question is what exactly happened between the Dark Ages (when the Tremere began their campaign against the Salubri) and the modern era when this strange, screwed up magical number of exactly seven 8th generation vampires engaging in a series of forced ritualized diableries show up. How did that transition occur and why?<br /> <br /> On the whole, the Children seem to be what they appear to be at first glance.<br /> <br /> The Salubri have so many strange facts about them, alternative interpretations for their behavior are screaming to be made. The most compelling alternative interpretation (to me) is that Saulot made arrangements for a secret core of the Salubri to survive and instructed loyal disciples to perpetuate themselves through diablerie (likely to throw their enemies off their track since they now have a new form/identity \u2013 this may be why we're told Salubri appear like all sorts of people \u2013 old men, children, teenagers, middle aged women \u2013 note that one thing these concepts have in common is that these people do not appear threatening and are very different from one another) in order to retain a sacred, magical number of them for an unknown reason in the Jyhad. The rest of the clan he left to its fate. In so far it is Salubri that are hunted, it is not the 8th generation Salubri mentors, but their 9th generation childer who can easily be sacrificed as they can always make another whom they will force to diablerize themselves (and thus allow their soul \u2013 which might be the soul of the original Salubri of this strange group of seven a thousand years ago \u2013 to take over their new host).<br /> <br /> Is that alternative interpretation correct? I have no idea. The Salubri might just be what they appear to be at first glance \u2013 a small group of people of very odd behavior who want to help people's souls. And there are easily lots of other interpretations as well. Ultimately, it's up the ST what is true in their chronicle.<br /> <br /> Neither group to me seems a good option for a PC. There are just too many things that would frustrate an ST or PC in portraying them right. Too much mystery that would need to be expose, too many restrictions on PC actions. These groups should be resticted to NPCs and specific plots.<br /> <br /> Once you take into consideration, the considerable differences between the Salubri and Children of Osiris, it seems to me an ST would run very different plots with them even though they have a certain thing in common.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 11-26-2021, 10:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T23:55:01-07:00","id":1455483,"title":"","content":"The Salubri are interesting because they're this weird tiny group of vampires with a weird, unhealthy fixation of diablere and Golconda and in the modern nights they only make up an itty bitty fraction of their clan.<br /> <br /> And then you have the bloodlines which are totally different. The Salubri Anttitribu (Warrior caste) are part of the Sabbat and other than their blood, share nothing in common with the &quot;main&quot; branch. (not even their flaw) They don't pursue Golconda, they don't diablarize one another (or at least no more often than other Sabbat do). And then there's the Wu Zao (Watchers), another bloodline of the Salubri, who can be found throughout Asia and the Middle East. And they also aren't interested in Golconda. Instead they focus on gathering up mystical secrets and artifacts for some unknown purpose. And you even have a bloodline of the Salubri in Africa, the Nkulu Zao, and another 7 Salubri who are basically kept in gilded cages by the Assamites. <br /> <br /> At this point in time, between the Anttitribu, the Wu Zao and then Nkulu Zao and then the 7 Assamite Salubri, there's probably around 200-250 Salubri in the world, so the actual 7 Salubri would make up something like 2 or 3% of the entire clan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-24T03:43:36-07:00","id":1455501,"title":"","content":"To me the perfect idea of the Salubri is that... they were just another vampire clan, but generations of myth and mystery, as well as the part where they were destroyed by the highly divisive Tremere, has led to wild imaginations and conflicting ideas about whether they were saints too-good-for-this-world or absolute sinners. Most clans carry with them a strong culture, a sense of self-selection, and they often have weaknesses that reinforce certain ideas, but ultimately they're embracing individuals. your parents have a lot of influence over you, but they don't define you. <br /> <br /> The CoO on the otherhand is a cult. you join when you fit the mold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-24T14:17:07-07:00","id":1455532,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1455483#post1455483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And then you have the bloodlines which are totally different.</div> </div> </div> </div>For the purposes of this thread, a comparison of the Children and Osiris and Salubri in regards to their common themes of Humanity/being good guys, I think the anti-tribu are out of scope. It's why I said I was only addressing the original write up in the Players Guide because dealing with lore created afterwards just confuses things and wasn't relevant for <i>this</i> discussion. On another kind of Salubri thread, they'd be relevant.<br /> <br /> The presence of the antitribu and any other subclans are an artifact of the Revised era retconning anything they wanted in order to fit their specific railroad metaplot. I doubt highly if any of these concepts were ever imagined, much less intentioned, when the Salubri were first presented in the 1e Players Guide. This is one reason I say there's no actual continuity anymore in the setting. We're in an &quot;Earth-1&quot;, &quot;Earth-2&quot;, &quot;Earth-3&quot; type scenario in regards to the WoD (Vampire more than others since they have the most editions). You have to pick what version you like (or most likely create your own out of all the bits you like and ignore what you don't). That doesn't mean you're wrong if you really like the idea of Salubri as depicted now with all the retcons, changes, and baggage given to them. But I do think that is just one option for STs. People have different tastes.<br /> <br /> If an ST is going to use the Salubri, I think their best thing for them would be to decide what is the truth about Saulot, the Salubri, and Tremere's diablerie of him <i>in the context of their own chronicle setting</i>. What is true in <i>their</i> chronicle? What are the secrets and mysteries that could be revealed? What impact does it have on the Jyhad? That way, it's not just hokum mystery box BS. You can actually use the Salubri (or any of their bloodlines) in a way consistent with what you want to be true.<br /> <br /> The &quot;official&quot; version told in Revised should just be one <i>option</i>. Want to make him a good guy and his diablerie tragic and explain how the questionable things the Salubri do are actually OK? Go for it. Want to make Salubri a good guy who somehow went awry or his plan got botched along the way? That works too. Want to portray the truth about Salubri as being essentially unknowable, and have all these different bloodlines (one of which arguably is the Tremere themselves) arguing about which right? That can be interesting as well. And there's probably at least another five versions we could easily come up, and lots of creative STs out there who will come up with their own fascinating ideas.<br /> <br /> But if you're not interested in defining that mystery or secrets in your own chronicle, best to ignore the Salubri completely IMHO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-24T16:53:44-07:00","id":1455539,"title":"","content":"<b>Children of Osiris numbers</b><br /> <br /> Honestly, I think that it's also important to note the Children of Osiris are probably incredibly tiny. People tend to overestimate the size of Bloodlines and Clans because vampires are not a huge group so that any faction of them is noticeable. I'd suggest that maybe the number of Children of Osiris is around 30. Possibly less. It's a Blood Cult that is identified in THE HUNTER'S HUNTED but something clearly no one outside of the Setites have ever heard of.<br /> <br /> The Salubri may have more knowledge about them but I don't think the CoO are any more numerous.<br /> <br /> <b>The Salubri</b><br /> <br /> Contrary to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a>, I'd argue that you should never solve the riddle of Saulot. I actually think it was the biggest misstep of the Transylvania Chronicles because if you're playing a died in the wool Golconda worshiper then if the player character is uninterested in roleplaying a fall from grace, it's going to go over like a wet fart. I remember when I first introduced the idea and the Salubri character reacted with, &quot;Yeah, that's more propaganda.&quot; The heart of the Salubri is in the mystery and the faith in their view.<br /> <br /> At least, IMHO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 07-24-2021, 08:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-15T23:54:05-07:00","id":1464160,"title":"","content":"Since this thread I've been given to thinking about the Children of Osiris and how I want to portray them in my chronicles. I only had some vague ideas beforehand, but this thread developed a lot more details for me. So now I'm at the point of putting concrete ideas in my overall game setting. I find having some definite details helps me as an ST make better decisions during the game itself.<br /> <br /> First off, I'm using the idea that there are around several dozen Children of Osiris which closely matches CTPhipps idea that there are around thirty of them. You may view them as having more or less members than me. So where to put them?<br /> <br /> Clearly most members should be at the mysterious snowy mountain headquarters of the order somewhere in Nepal. I'm arbitrarily assigning a dozen members there including Khetamon, the Grand Undying King. These are probably most ancient vampires who personally knew Osiris, but there's probably younger vampires as well. But I think any permanent members dwelling there are still elders with younger vampires only temporarily there to be indoctrinated or advance in knowledge. Their temple there must have sufficient grounds to feed a herd of yaks or so, but there are probably no humans. Still I imagine the &quot;monastery&quot; does have dealings with some village of humans to be porters and other servants as needed.<br /> <br /> But there must be other temples as well. I believe the Children need to operate globally as they both recruit new members on occasion, destroy Setites when they can, and conduct other operations. I imagine the temples likely stay in contact with one another through judicious use of Astral Projection. Any sufficiently old elder would likely have Auspex at such levels. Keeping with the monastic theme, there is probably some kind of elder &quot;abbot&quot; that keeps in touch with Nepal and teaches/administers the teachings/disciplines of Osiris and oversees operations. The abbot would need to be very old and very trusted by the Grand Undying King. He is likely assisted by a more active second in command who would conduct field operations and deal with the younger members.<br /> <br /> I think such temples would probably comprise around 4-6 members. That leaves 4-5 temples in total that need to cover the rest of the world. Each temple would likely be responsible for a geographic area. They'd run any reconnaissance, potential recruitment, and killing Setites and other enemies in that region. I think such temples would follow the theme or isolated mountain monasteries, but preferably would also not be too far from a place that would be conducive to travel elsewhere. They want isolation, but to a certain amount they need to be able to travel long distances to conduct operations so being somewhat close to a city with a port, airport, railroad, or road network helps. &quot;Close&quot; of course is merely relative.<br /> <br /> There should be at least one in the Western Hemisphere, possibly two (one in North America and possibly one in South America). So I think a North American temple would be located in one of the mountain ranges in California not too far from San Francisco. Maybe somewhere in the Sierra Nevada. If in South America, it'd have to be located in the Andes. Not sure of the best place for it, but currently considering Peru somewhere not too far from Lima.<br /> <br /> That leaves two or three temples in the Eastern Hemisphere which is a lot of territory to cover. I imagine at minimum they need a western and eastern temple.<br /> <br /> Since the Grand Temple in Nepal wouldn't run direct operations, it makes sense they'd need one for eastern Eurasia elsewhere in India. So I'll put another temple somewhere in the Western Ghats. It's not that far from several of India's port cities or from the Golconda area that may attract vampire pilgrims for obvious reasons. It'd be well placed to control operations in South or Eastern Asia.<br /> <br /> I'm putting another temple in France, perhaps the French Jura or Alps. This has several advantages. One is that it is not far from Switzerland which is where Horus lives and rules the mummies. Since I assume the Grand Undying King deals with Horus, having people close by to fulfill missions would be handy. Second being in France puts it in contact with Paris which was the first European nation to go crazy about Egyptology after Napoleon's invasion. That would be useful. Lastly, it's the home of the lupine Sept of the Sun's Glory which is heavy into vampire hunting. Ease of contact with some Garou there could serve their needs quite well. You can cover Europe from there and possibly North Africa and the Middle East.<br /> <br /> If I was going to place a third temple, I'm leaning towards some snow capped mountain in Turkey or the Caucasus. It's much closer to Egypt and can keep an eye on there. Plus if they kill any vampires, most vampires will associate mountain dwelling vampire assassins in Turkey with the Assamites, not the Children of Osiris. Another possibility would be the Simien mountains of Ethiopia. It'd be perfect in many ways except that it's historically been very isolated and therefore difficult to use as a base to conduct operations. But the connection to the Nile and therefore Egypt would make a nice base to watch the Setites. Just need to watch out for those cataracts. Maybe it'd be more viable if they make good use of allies among the Silent Striders?<br /> <br /> These temples aren't static. I imagine over the five thousand years or so that the Children of Osiris existed, they've had established and abandoned many. This is just what they may have right now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-20T06:41:24-07:00","id":1464583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris?p=1464160#post1464160\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since this thread I've been given to thinking about the Children of Osiris and how I want to portray them in my chronicles. I only had some vague ideas beforehand, but this thread developed a lot more details for me. So now I'm at the point of putting concrete ideas in my overall game setting. I find having some definite details helps me as an ST make better decisions during the game itself.<br /> <br /> First off, I'm using the idea that there are around several dozen Children of Osiris which closely matches CTPhipps idea that there are around thirty of them. You may view them as having more or less members than me. So where to put them?<br /> <br /> Clearly most members should be at the mysterious snowy mountain headquarters of the order somewhere in Nepal. I'm arbitrarily assigning a dozen members there including Khetamon, the Grand Undying King. These are probably most ancient vampires who personally knew Osiris, but there's probably younger vampires as well. But I think any permanent members dwelling there are still elders with younger vampires only temporarily there to be indoctrinated or advance in knowledge. Their temple there must have sufficient grounds to feed a herd of yaks or so, but there are probably no humans. Still I imagine the &quot;monastery&quot; does have dealings with some village of humans to be porters and other servants as needed.<br /> <br /> But there must be other temples as well. I believe the Children need to operate globally as they both recruit new members on occasion, destroy Setites when they can, and conduct other operations. I imagine the temples likely stay in contact with one another through judicious use of Astral Projection. Any sufficiently old elder would likely have Auspex at such levels. Keeping with the monastic theme, there is probably some kind of elder &quot;abbot&quot; that keeps in touch with Nepal and teaches/administers the teachings/disciplines of Osiris and oversees operations. The abbot would need to be very old and very trusted by the Grand Undying King. He is likely assisted by a more active second in command who would conduct field operations and deal with the younger members.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Milarepa\" target=\"_blank\">Something</a> i'd say definitely relates to the subject, along with <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Caerns:_Places_of_Power#Stargazers:_Tibet\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-20T13:17:31-07:00","id":1464629,"title":"","content":"Thanks for that tidbit. While I have Caerns: Places of Power, there's a lot I haven't read or only skimmed through so I was unaware the Children of Osiris were even mentioned. There's definitely some things to ponder.<br /> <br /> First, the Stargazer caern is in Tibet while the main Osiran temple is in Nepal. So Milarepa seems to be more like a hermit monk rather than living at one of the temples. So there are probably other Children of Osiris like this as well. He also travels discretely seeking knowledge, so he's not always isolated.<br /> <br /> Second, he's a 5th generation vampire over 900 years old. Unknown when he joined the Children, but he's of rather potent blood for being embraced at such a late date. In the caern history section we're told he helped the Garou Justinian to establish the caern in 1083 and helped destroy a Nexus Crawler. That would imply that the vampire joined the Children very early on. A quick look in Wikipedia says &quot;Milarepa&quot; was a prominent Tibetan Buddhist monk yogi right around 900 years ago who, like the character here, was a poet. It's highly likely the original author meant this historical figure to be this vampire since Caerns is an early product when lots of historical people were actual supernatural creatures in the game. Nowadays we'd probably say the character was &quot;inspired&quot; by the person. <br /> <br /> Not mentioned in the White Wolf wiki article but in the book is that Milarepa's primary Disciplines are Bardo, Auspex, Fortitude, and Chimestry. So it's most likely he's Ravnos. If he is based on the historical character, we can likely discount that he committed any diablerie post-embrace because he was already a revered spiritual figure in life (although before he committed murder earlier in life before he repented and embarked on his spiritual life). Since it's unlikely a Child of Osiris would have embraced him directly, there's various ways we could go to provide him a real origin. One that I like is that a Ravnos vampire cruelly embraced him to punish/corrupt the man, but the Children of Osiris moved quickly to provide him guidance. Maybe he stays near the Stargazer caern because the Children want to preserve the sept as allies. (There can't be many 4th generation Ravnos running around that could sire him around the year 1000. Maybe it was Vestgeir, the fabled Ravnos Methusaleh who was instrumental in forming the Sabbat from the anarchs who rejected the Convention of Thorns? Are there other known candidates?)<br /> <br /> Third, we know he uses ghouls (most likely SG kinfolk, but not explicitly said to be) as two protect his cave during the day. I would not have thought the Children of Osiris would create ghouls. They are called &quot;novices&quot; in the book indicating that Milarepa is teaching them in the ways of spiritualism. However, the level 6 power of Bardo, Boon of Anubis, protects a mortal from becoming a vampire. However, if a vampire attempts the embrace he is &quot;considered&quot; to be a ghoul. So I would rule these are not &quot;true&quot; ghouls, but mortals effectively considered to be ghouls as a result of being protected against becoming vampires, but some other vampire attempted to embrace them. I could see the Children bestowing the Boon of Anubis on many mortals who help them. So likely these novices were part of some operation of the Children that went bad. An enemy vampire tried to embrace them, and this became their new state. Later on the the Children of Osiris allowed them to join the order and they were reassigned to help Milarepa. It's not exactly sure what it means to be such a &quot;ghoul&quot;, and I noticed in V20 the same Bardo power drops the idea that are &quot;considered&quot; to be a ghoul. It's an artifact of the original power's description in Hunter's Hunted. So in new rules, these two guards likely aren't ghouls, just very trusted mortal allies. Their likely kinfolk status indicates that there is a very close relationship between the SG caern and the Children of Osiris. It's likely both SG Garou and kinfolk from this sept might assist the Osirans in killing certain vampires.<br /> <br /> Fourth, we're told that while it's unknown if Milarepa has achieved Golconda, he acts as though he has.<br /> <br /> So this actually gives us some good ideas of how the Children operate. The Children don't seem opposed to Golconda which goes back to a debate earlier in the thread. We also have some ideas of mortal agents helping the children out, and often protected by the 6th rank Bardo power of Boon of Anubis. That makes complete sense. It would also indicate any major temple that conducts operations would be headed by an Osiran who is at least 7th generation. We can also see that some Children do live alone as hermits, but probably not TOO far from an established temple. That might boost their total numbers up a bit. And that they can count on a Stargazer sept as allies in some capacity even if it is only done through Milarepa alone because of his unique ties to the sept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-22T08:15:28-07:00","id":1464873,"title":"","content":"This thread did helped me a lot. Unlike most I actually do use both the CoO and the Salubri with some frequency. And I played as Salubri too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}